      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subject: Key Reminders for Optimal Ergonomic Workspaces for Remote Employees\n\nDear Team,\n\nAs we continue to embrace our work-from-home model, it’s paramount that we prioritize our health and well-being. To aid in this, I'm extending specific and actionable advice about ergonomics to help combat aches and pain associated with extended periods of sitting.\n\n- Monitor: Your computer screen should be at or just below eye level and about an arm's length away. Consider a sliding tray if you're unable to position the monitor correctly.\n- Chair and Feet: Maintain your feet flat on the ground or on a footrest, and adjust the chair so that you sit with your back fully supported. Remember to keep a slight curve in your lower back.\n- Keyboard and Mouse: Your keyboard should allow your elbows to stay close to your body, with wrists straight and in line with your hands, using your forearm rather than your wrists to type or click.\n- Break It Up: Incorporate breaks into your routine. A short, 5-minute stretch or a change of scenery every hour can make a significant difference.\n\nIf your work setup does not meet these criteria, you may experience symptoms such as persistent wrist discomfort, back stiffness, or neck pain. Addressing these ergonom",
      "source": "target",
